Instead, as part of the bargain that spared him the death penalty, he admitted to those uncharged crimes in open court.

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:27.700
That August, after sitting silent for nearly half a century, he was brought into a Sacramento courtroom to face the survivors and families who had waited forty years to look him in the eye.

00:20:28.300 --> 00:20:31.400
In August 2020, the formal legal proceedings came to a close.

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:37.300
Joseph DeAngelo was sentenced to more than twelve life terms, ensuring he will spend the rest of his life behind bars.

00:20:37.300 --> 00:20:43.400
The 74-year-old former police officer sat in court and was confronted directly by his victims and their family members.

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:49.200
He had pleaded guilty to twenty-six counts in total: thirteen of first-degree murder, and thirteen of kidnapping.

00:20:49.200 --> 00:21:03.700
The statute of limitations had long expired on the dozens of rapes he was accused of committing, but his guilty pleas provided an official resolution on the record, accounting for a violent spree that left at least thirteen dead, more than fifty raped, and over a hundred homes ransacked.

00:21:04.300 --> 00:21:10.900
Looking back across the decades, it is clear how deeply the technological limits of the 1970s and 1980s shaped this investigation.

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:16.900
Law enforcement agencies were siloed, working without cell phones, surveillance networks, or modern DNA databases.

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:27.800
A cunning offender could exploit those invisible county borders, leaving detectives to chase contradictory clues across Northern and Southern California while believing they were hunting entirely different men.

00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:32.300
In Visalia, they chased a burglar who scattered undergarments and shot Claude Snelling.

00:21:32.300 --> 00:21:37.600
In Sacramento, they searched for a prowler who pried open windows and murdered Brian and Katie Maggiore.

00:21:37.600 --> 00:21:41.100
Along the southern coast, they looked for a killer tying diamond knots.

00:21:41.100 --> 00:21:44.800
The case files were separated by hundreds of miles and years of dead ends.

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:49.700
The breakthrough only came when investigators stepped outside conventional tools.

00:21:49.700 --> 00:22:02.100
By pulling pristine genetic material frozen by a forward-thinking pathologist in 1980 and utilizing investigative genetic genealogy through GEDmatch, they navigated from distant cousins down to a single door handle and a discarded tissue.

00:22:02.100 --> 00:22:09.900
That science solved a forty-year cold case, but it also ignited an ongoing national debate about privacy, government reach, and familial DNA searching.

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:16.900
Studies noted that vast portions of the population could now be identified through public databases by relatives they had never even met.

00:22:17.500 --> 00:22:26.000
The file is closed on Joseph DeAngelo, yet the forensic precedent his capture established continues to reshape criminal investigations nationwide.

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:26.800
Read the file.

00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:28.300
Form your own conclusion.

00:22:28.300 --> 00:22:29.500
That's it for this one.

00:22:29.500 --> 00:22:31.300
Thanks for hanging out with me.

